> ===
> Long-term risks require long-term planning: Bangladesh case study
> 
> Bangladesh has been identified as one of the countries with the greatest risks from climate change. It has a vulnerable population exposed to multiple extreme hazards, including  frequent, intense, persistent, and erratic cyclones, floods, droughts, and slow-onset impacts such as sea level rise, ocean acidification, and salinity intrusion. 
> To take one hazard, by mid-century under climate change, Bangladesh faces a quadrupling the risk of extreme rainfall events during the monsoon, as the monsoon becomes more erratic (Fahad et al., 2024; Kamruzzaman et al., 2024).
> These risks are already manifesting, with six flooding events that each affected over 5 million people in Bangladesh over the last decade (Delforge 2025). Studies have also highlighted the disproportionate impact on women and the elderly, with potential for food insecurity and health risks (Hazrana et al., 2025). These events also have very long-term consequences, with lasting health, educational and economic consequences from events like the 1970 cyclone still observed in affected populations over 40 years later (Eskander and Barbier, 2022).
> Like many developing countries, Bangladesh aims to integrate the multiple demands of climate adaptation, economic development and climate mitigation (Ayers et al. 2017). Bangladesh is notable in its activities to mainstream adaptation into medium-term development, including under its 8th Five-Year Plan.
> Climate-resilient development pathways (CRDP) has emerged as a central paradigm for doing this. CRDP recognises the role of forward-looking planning of actions when engaging in adaptation to risks that will continue evolving for decades.
> Bangladesh has developed a state-of-the-art example of CRDP in the Bangladesh Delta Plan 2100 (BDP, 2100). BDP 2100 is a comprehensive, multi-hazard and multi-sector plan, with the mission to “ensure long-term water and food security, economic growth, and environmental sustainability while effectively reducing vulnerability to natural disasters and building resilience to climate change and other delta challenges through robust, adaptive, and integrated strategies, and equitable water governance” (Arfanuzzaman, 2024). If fully implemented, BDP 2100 is expected to eliminate a 5.6% decline in GDP that Bangladesh would otherwise experience (World Bank, 2021).
> Bangladesh has centred the national-level planning processes itself, producing significant documents such as the National Action Plan for Clean Cooking (2019), Sustainable Finance Policy (2020), National Strategy on Internal Displacement Management (2021), National Adaptation Plan (2022), Sector Action Plan on Environment and Climate Change (2023) and Mujib Climate Prosperity Plan (2024).
> Together, the BDP 2100, National Adaptation Plan and Mujib Climate Prosperity Plan lay out a need for US$357.7 billion in funding (Arfanuzzaman, 2024). This is significantly greater than what is possible for the Bangladesh government alone, which invests US$4.7 billion in development, with about 50% of that provided by international aid (EDC, 2020).
> Rather than focusing on the shortfall, Bangladesh used its planning process as a key component of its effective approach to securing international finance. For example, this likely contributed to their receiving the largest disbursements from the UNFCCC Adaptation Fund in South Asia (Arfanuzzaman, 2024), and one of the highest levels of low-interest credit for adaptation among lower-income countries (WRI, 2025).
> Bangladesh’s approach to planning offers elements of a blueprint to follow their success. First, Bangladesh uses advanced, internationally recognised frameworks, like CRDP, to construct integrated perspectives. They design public–private partnerships, with project selection processes and government co-funding, as part of their plans (EDC, 2020). And, finally, they have created a dedicated adaptation fund, the Bangladesh Climate Change Resilience Fund, to support these projects.
> While extensive evaluations of the benefits of this process are unavailable, WRI (2025) highlights the Weather and Climate Services Regional Project, which is estimated to avoid $93 million in losses and provide $10 million in economic benefits, producing an economic rate of return of 75%.
> As an integrated approach of conceptualisation, engagement, funding and implementation, Bangladesh’s planning process itself is an excellent example of adaptation in action.
